The Human-English defines a continuous lattice to be a complete lattice \(D\) in which every \(y\) equals \(\bigsqcup\{x \in D \mid x \ll y\}\), and says that every element is the least upper bound of the elements way below it. `ContinuousLattice` is that definition, a `Prop` class. The field `out` stores the least-upper-bound statement, and the field `sSup_wayBelow` is the supremum equation, filled from `out` because a least upper bound equals the supremum. `WayBelow.interpolate` proves that \(a \ll c\) produces some \(b\) with \(a \ll b\) and \(b \ll c\). The set of elements way below something way below \(c\) is directed and nonempty, and the continuous-lattice equation makes its supremum \(c\), so \(a\) is way below some member of that set. **Theorem:** partial — the definition is a definition, and interpolation is proved. **Proof:** yes — interpolation follows from directedness of the way-below set and the supremum equation.
